# Handoff and Documentation (load-bearing, non-negotiable)

Projects are worked on by multiple developers across multiple machines and agent
sessions. Documentation is part of finishing the task, not optional cleanup.
These files live in the repo being worked on. If they do not exist yet, CREATE
them; do not skip the step because they're absent.

## On EVERY session start, before changing anything
Read these if present (create on first write if absent):
1. `docs/HANDOFF.md` — current state of the world.
2. `docs/DECISIONS.md` — standing architectural and security decisions.
3. `docs/OPEN-ISSUES.md` — known problems and blockers.
If the task conflicts with anything in them, stop and surface it first.

## On EVERY task completion, before the task is done
Append a new entry to the TOP of the log in `docs/HANDOFF.md` (create the file
if it doesn't exist) with:
- Requested — the actual ask, one or two lines.
- Done — concrete changes: files, commands, config, infra.
- Issues — anything broken, incomplete, deferred, or risky.
- Stand / next — current state and the single next action if any.
- Who / where — developer or agent identity, machine, branch@commit if known.
- Timestamp — ISO 8601 UTC.
Then refresh a "Current snapshot" block at the top of `docs/HANDOFF.md`.

Also update when relevant (create if absent):
- `docs/DECISIONS.md` — new standing decisions future work must respect.
- `docs/OPEN-ISSUES.md` — add new issues; mark resolved ones resolved, do not
  delete.
- `docs/LEARNINGS.md` — durable lessons from the run (e.g. "slices editing a
  shared type must serialize, not parallelize"), written by an agent other than
  the one that made the mistake, so review is independent. Read this at session
  start too, and let it change how you decompose future work.

## Hard constraints
- A task is NOT complete until the `docs/HANDOFF.md` entry is written. No
  exceptions.
- Never delete history from these docs. Append and mark status. History is the
  point.
- Never write secrets, tokens, credentials, or key material into any doc or rule
  file. Reference the secret's location only, never its value.
- Keep entries factual and specific. No vague optimism, no filler.
- If a change spans multiple repos or submodules, note the cross-repo dependency.

## Why this exists
State lives in the repo, not in any one person's head or any one AI session.
That is what makes knowledge survive across sessions, machines, and developers.
